IMAGE QUALITY IS A RELEASE-BLOCKING REQUIREMENT.

This is not a cosmetic detail.

Users may pay for BlueMind, so opening an attached image in fullscreen must never produce an obviously blurry or degraded result when the original image is sharp.

I want maximum available source quality.

If the original attachment is 4K/high-resolution, the fullscreen viewer must use that original high-resolution asset. Do NOT downsample it to thumbnail resolution and then upscale it.

Do not artificially claim “4K” or upscale a low-resolution image and call it fixed. The goal is:

preserve the original image resolution and visible detail with zero unnecessary quality loss.

If the existing image-preview implementation/library cannot reliably render the original attachment at high quality with zoom and pan, you may replace that preview implementation or use a proven native iOS image-viewing solution/library.

Before adding any dependency:

* prefer native UIKit/SwiftUI APIs if they can provide full-resolution rendering;
* if a third-party library is genuinely necessary, choose a maintained, lightweight solution compatible with the project;
* do not add a heavy dependency just to hide the underlying problem;
* explain why the dependency is necessary before relying on it.

Investigate the complete image path:

original Photos asset / selected file
→ attachment representation
→ composer thumbnail
→ preview state
→ fullscreen decoder
→ displayed pixels.

At no point should the fullscreen viewer accidentally reuse the composer thumbnail or a compressed intermediate representation.

Verify using the SAME image side-by-side:

Photos app vs BlueMind fullscreen preview.

BlueMind should appear essentially as sharp as the original at normal viewing scale. When zooming, it should reveal the detail available in the original source rather than enlarged thumbnail pixels.

Log the actual pixel dimensions and file/source being rendered so this is proven, not assumed.

⸻

X BUTTON IS ALSO MANDATORY

The top-left glass X button must be fully functional, not decorative.

On tap:

→ dismiss the fullscreen preview immediately
→ return to the existing composer
→ keep the image attached
→ preserve draft text and all other attachments.

Give the X a proper native touch target and verify on a real iPhone that the tap is received.

If another overlay/gesture layer is intercepting the touch, fix the hit-testing hierarchy rather than adding another fake button.

Do not mark this task complete until BOTH conditions are verified on-device:

1. The fullscreen image is sharp and uses the original/highest-resolution source.
2. The X button actually closes the preview every time.
